::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the mean of 5, 7, 9, 11.
{
=A. 8#Correct. Add to get 32, then divide by 4 to get 8.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the median of 4, 10, 6, 12, 8.
{
=A. 8#Correct. Order the data as 4, 6, 8, 10, 12; the middle value is 8.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the range of 18, 22, 25, 30.
{
=A. 12#Correct. Range is greatest minus least: 30 - 18 = 12.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which measure of center uses every value in the data set?
{
=A. mean#Correct. The mean includes every value in the sum before division.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which measure of center is the middle value after ordering?
{
=A. median#Correct. The median is defined by the middle position in ordered data.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which value is an outlier in 12, 13, 14, 15, 40?
{
=A. 40#Correct. The value 40 is far from the cluster from 12 to 15.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
What does a larger range usually mean?
{
=A. greater spread#Correct. A larger range means the highest and lowest values are farther apart.
~B. mode#Mode is the most frequent value, so it does not match this question.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Why can the mean be misleading with an outlier?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
=B. The outlier can pull the mean toward an extreme value.#Correct. The mean uses every value, including extreme values.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the mean of 2, 4, 6.
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
=B. 4#Correct. The sum is 12, and 12 / 3 = 4.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the median of 20, 30, 40, 50.
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
=B. 35#Correct. For an even number of values, average the two middle values: 30 and 40.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Find the range of 3, 3, 7, 12.
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
=B. 9#Correct. The greatest value is 12 and the least is 3, so the range is 9.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which statistic describes the value that appears most often?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
=B. mode#Correct. The mode is the most frequent value.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
A data set has values close together. Which word describes it?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
=B. low variability#Correct. Values close together have less spread.
~C. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which summary is resistant to an extreme high value?
{
~A. mean#Mean uses all values and can be pulled by an extreme value, so it is not the most resistant choice here.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
=C. median#Correct. The median depends on position, so it is less affected by one extreme value.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
What does interquartile range measure?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
=C. spread of the middle half#Correct. IQR measures the distance from Q1 to Q3.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
A data set has no repeated values. What is true about the mode?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
=C. There may be no mode.#Correct. If no value repeats, there may be no mode.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which step must happen before finding the median?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
=C. order the data#Correct. The median is based on the middle position after ordering.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which measure uses only the greatest and least values?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
=C. range#Correct. Range is calculated from only the maximum and minimum.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
What does a cluster in a dot plot show?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
=C. many values close together#Correct. A cluster is a group of nearby data values.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which value best describes the typical score when data are symmetric with no outliers?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~C. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
=D. mean#Correct. The mean is useful for symmetric data without outliers.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
A low score far from the rest of the data is called what?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~C. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
=D. outlier#Correct. An outlier is far away from the main group of values.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
What is Q3 in a data set?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~C. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
=D. the third quartile#Correct. Q3 marks the upper quartile of ordered data.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
What should an interpretation of mean include?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~C. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
=D. context and units#Correct. A mean should be explained using what the data measure.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Two classes have the same median but different ranges. What differs?
{
~A. median#Median is the middle value after ordering, not the value that appears most often.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~C. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
=D. variability#Correct. Range measures spread, so different ranges mean different variability.
}
::MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L02_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which measure is best for the middle value of ordered test scores?
{
~A. mean#Mean is a balance point, not the middle ordered value.
~B. maximum#Maximum is only the greatest value, not the requested summary.
~C. sample size#Sample size is the number of data values, not the requested measure.
=D. median#Correct. The median gives the middle ordered score.
}


